Division is not commutative and not associative: in general $a \div b \neq b \div a$ and $(a \div b) \div c \neq a \div (b \div c)$. What is true, whenever all the quotients involved are exact (whole numbers), is:

1. $(a \div b) \div c = a \div (b \times c)$;

2. $(a \times k) \div (b \times k) = a \div b$ for $k \neq 0$, used when $a \div b$ is known to be exact (then so is the left side, and conversely);

3. $(p + q) \div b = p \div b + q \div b$ when $p$ and $q$ are multiples of $b$, and $(p - q) \div b = p \div b - q \div b$ when moreover $p \ge q$.

